ok at this um  we can first see that if we look at the  columns  there are the the first two columns here  are the critical difference  divided by the within reagent lot  standard deviation and then the second  the second table there  is the standard deviation  of the total the  within the laboratory standard deviation  divided by  the within reagent lot standard  deviation  so those are what we use to actually  index the row and then the other columns  are based on our  rejection limit so we're going to use  0.9.8.7  and so that's how we select which column  are we going to actually use so  let's take a quick look um  you need to actually compute those two  ratios  first where we've got again the ratio  of the critical change to analytical  precision  um and basically the uh  the higher that this is the lower  number of samples you're going to need  and then you've got the ratio  of your standard deviation  within the laboratory divided by the  within  reagent lot standard deviation  so that's really that's a  a ratio of repeatability to analytical  precision  and the smaller that that is  um the higher the number of samples that  you're going to need  so we use that combination is what's  used  to index the row that you're going to  use  within that within that appendix  okay so the way that we do this  is you first find a section that  matches your cd to swrl ratio  and then when if you have a value that's  between  two values you're going to use the next  lowest  value and then you find the row  that matches your sr to swrl  value and and you  once you've got that then you go across  to find the column that corresponds with  your rejection limit  so the number outside the parenthesis  is the number of samples required um the  first number inside  the parenthesis is the the probability  of a false  rejection a false positive result  and then the second is the probability  of error detection  so let's take a quick look let me just  so um let's  let's see how we actually use this all  right so in this example  um i'm using an example based on ast  with two sample concentrations um  we've got 40 and 200  and you can see the corresponding  critical differences  and here we have the uh  the different the critical difference  and the swrl  and the the sr  and we've decided that for this analyte  we're going to use a rejection criteria  of 0.7 and then we've gone on ahead and  we've computed  the ratios that we need to use in order  to actually  find within the table  just how many samples do we need  so let's look at what that looks like  okay now this is  a little hard to see but  if you take a look here the ratios that  we're looking for  are 7.7 and 0.62  and so when we look this up  um we're going to go one down  so that puts us that puts us  in for the first one so this is a  section  of the table then we look at 0.62  and where so that gives us the row of  0.6  and then we select the column that's  telling us  okay what is the experiment design that  we need  and it's telling us that in this case we  need just one sample  now the samples that we're talking about  here are the patient samples  so for the design of this crossover  study  for ast we would need one  sample that's going to be near 40.  [Music]  and then we would do the same thing on  our second concentration so in this case  at for 200 we go through the same  process  and we at 4.9  we find the one that's next down which  is 4.5  and then with our with our  sr to swrl ratio it's  0.32 so one down from that is going to  be 0.3  so we basically round down when we're  trying to determine exactly which row to  use  and in this case it's telling us that we  need two samples  near 200. so that then  gives us um  the rea the sample design  okay and then this is what  now that we know that we've got we're  gonna need two specimens  uh for the the higher concentration and  one specimen for the  the lower concentration this is what  that might look like  so we can see that the current  lot result um we have 62  at the for the lower concentration  and then with the the new reagent lot  that we're looking at  it's 62.6 and so  the difference between those is 0.6  and in this case you know we want to  actually take a look at what's the  average  difference and it's 0.6  then we can do the same thing on the  the higher concentration where now in  this case we're using  two samples and with those two samples  when we do the same thing we see that we  have a difference of  minus 1.4 and minus 11.  so when we average those the average  just  difference is minus 6.25  well our rejection limits are 7  and 14. so our  differences are less than the rejection  limits  and so we're going to go ahead and  accept those  and so this is um how you  you design the study that you're gonna  use  and uh once you have done this type of a  study  once you've done the the design of the  study  you can use this over and over again  so while it does take some initial work  to design this type of study once it's  done for your test system and  ideally this is done uh  when you adopt uh the the test the test  system so when you're doing the  validation  of your test method you want to  do a this design at the same time  um but if that didn't take place well  then you you need to do the design and  what you have to design  you can use it over and over again the  only thing that you may need to  when you may need to revisit it is if  you have  issues with trying to locate patient  samples that are going to be in the  concentration range  that the study is designed for  so all of this is coming from clsi  document ep 26  and that is what i would recommend that  you take a look at  for determining how to do reagent  crossover studies  thank you very much  thank you john for that great  presentation we will now  start the live q a portion of the  webinar if you have any questions you'd  like to ask either nico or john  please do so now just click on that ask  a question box located on the far  left of your screen so let's get started  gentlemen it looks like we already have  some great questions  coming in addressed for both of you so  our first question  goes to you john our audience member  says  do i really need to use patient  specimens  for a reagent crossover study  yes you do and the reason for that  is that uh you know as i as i noted in  my presentation  there is this possible matrix effect  where the qc specimens can sometimes  behave  differently than patient specimens  and so you do actually need to take a  look and confirm  that what is the the performance with  respect to patient specimens  and unfortunately you know  we've had reports from labs that  you know if you simply rely on only qc  results they you can have shifts when it  comes to  the patient means um that  are caused by a new reagent lot number  that you might not detect  just from qc alone and so because of  that  you do need to actually look at both qc  and patient specimens  thank you so much john our next question  is for you nico  how often do i need to check my mean and  sd values  if they're still appropriate  okay so after our crossover when we  initially set our targets  then i would wait until we have  something like at least  60 to like 90 data points uh before we  would look at them again  and and then we can evaluate if our  targets are still  uh appropriate if something would have  shifted a bit up for your target for  example those are things you can then  evaluate  throughout the life of the control it is  something which you would probably do  like in your monthly review and i really  like using  uh the levi jennings chart for that  because you can clearly see  if your results start trending upwards  or trending downwards  if that would need a change it's also  important to see if you can  rather fix it first with for example a  calibration or with maintenance  then just continuously changing your  targets that is also another  the best practice but normally you  wouldn't check  more than once a month to see if  something  is behaving differently also remember  that yeah the most critical points are  reagent block changes  or calibrated law changes things like  that that can have an effect on on your  targets  thank you so much nico and i want to  thank our audience for these great  questions  coming in we'll move back to you john  should we also establish new means for  each  different qc or reagent shipment  so what you need to do is take a look to  see  if uh if you're other if that's  necessary  so reagent lots a new reagent lot  may or may not require you to change  means  and what you need to do is simply  evaluate  your qc materials on the new lot number  and take a look to see if you know how  different are they  from the old lot number and if they're  very different  then you need to basically do the same  approach basic do another  uh qc crossover uh if  if there is a significant difference in  the performance  thank you so much now nico an audience  member wants to know  can i establish my mean value in  multiple measurements  on the same day yes good question  you you can if if you would want to if  it's really necessary  um with this new guidance of clsi  with just using 10 data points in 10  different days  it already shortens or crossover study  if you really don't  have enough material to to last those 10  days for example and you need to go  faster you can  just remember that if you go faster and  you do multiple  measurements in one day there could be a  bit less variation  in those results so you could have a bit  more  uh you could see it a little later for  example so it  it's it's you can do it but just make  sure that after a longer period of time  you re-evaluate and you look at it  thank you nico and here's a follow-up  question about outliers  do i have to remove outliers from my  crossover period  so i think i mentioned this in the  presentation so we would always look at  our current uh quality control to see if  there is something out of control or  there is a problem then we will remove  that result  from the crossover study but it could  happen  that you have maybe a random error or  an outlier just in your new lot when  you're doing your crossover study that  didn't happen in the other one so if you  would see a clear and flagrant  outlier in those 10 data points in your  crossover  you could remove it and add an  additional data point  so you could take a look normally the  chance is smaller to have something like  that  but if it happens you could actually  remove it yes  thank you so much nico we have time for  just one more question  john this one's for you how can i tell  if there has been a change in  performance with my new lot of rea  agents well  that's actually what we have to do here  is that the way we can tell  if there is a change um and really  what we're concerned about is not just  whether or not there's a change  what we're concerned about is is there a  change  that is large enough that it's going to  impact  the the way that we use test results  for our patients and so it's a critical  change  that that we're trying to detect and and  the way that we do that  is with this uh reagent crossover study  design  so we need to take the  the number of samples that our design is  requiring  us to use um and we're going to take  patient samples run them on the current  lot number  and run them on the candidate lot number  and then look at those differences and  see if those differences  are acceptable or not um and so  we really want to do that before we  actually start  using the new lot number of reagents  because if we don't do that check then  we are going to introduce  those changes without  without realizing that that that's  happened  and and that can be that can present  a a dangerous set of circumstances for  the laboratory  um and in particular it it can  potentially have a negative impact on  on our patients and so that's why we  actually have to do  this reagent crossover um  when you have a new lot number if  somebody else  that has not done that study  gentlemen i want to thank you again for  your time today and i want to thank you  for your important research  and before we go i want to thank our  audience for joining us  today and for their interesting  questions questions we did not have time  for today  will be addressed by our speakers and  posted in the event  resources by the end of next week and  this webcast  will be available on demand in the  auditorium from friday on  if you found this information helpful  please share it with colleagues for the  next webinar on april  7th we will have a panel discussion  where you can ask  all of your questions about quality  control  four qc experts will be our panelists  and they will answer your qc questions  please stay tuned and watch  your email inbox for the invitation and  you will also be able to submit your  questions up front  to the panel until next time stay safe  and stay healthy  thank you and bye


